Take the faces' adaptive integrals side by side, and add them in order.

What it changes
  BRepGProp::SurfaceProperties and VolumeProperties with an Eps integrate
  a shape face by face (BRepGProp_Sinert, BRepGProp_Vinert), each face
  about the same point, and add each result to the shape's as they go.
  The adaptive integral of a B-spline face is milliseconds, and a part in
  hundreds of them is integrated on one thread.

  One face's integral reads nothing of another's: the loops reuse one
  BRepGProp_Face, one BRepGProp_Domain and one integrator, and each is
  loaded afresh per face. So with Eps < 1 the faces are listed in the
  loop's own order, integrated with OSD_Parallel, each with a face,
  a domain and an integrator of its own, and then added to the shape's
  properties in that order: the same additions of the same numbers one
  thread makes. A shape with a face that has no surface, or no wire (the
  loop would hand such a face the domain of the face before it), and a
  shape of one face, take the loop as it was. The non-adaptive calls and
  the triangulation paths are untouched.

How it was measured
  See vendor/occt-sys/PARCAD-CHANGES.md.

Upstream status
  Not yet reported.

